<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Albert, Duke of Prussia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1490">1490</a>–<a href="/years/1568">1568</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Albert of Prussia, Albrecht of Brandenburg-Ansbach, Albert of Brandenburg-Ansbach, Albrecht of Prussia · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">House of Hohenzollern</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Albert, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, a Hohenzollern of the Ansbach line, was the last Grand Master of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> in Prussia. In 1525, advised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a>, he converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> and secularised the Order's state as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-prussia">Duchy of Prussia</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Polish crown</a>, the first Protestant state. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Samland and Pomesania handed him their secular powers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albert-duke-of-prussia.jpg" alt="Albert, Duke of Prussia" width="320" height="439" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lucas Cranach the Elder · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lucas_Cranach_d.%C3%84._-_Bildnis_des_Markgrafen_Albrecht_von_Brandenburg-Ansbach_(Herzog_Anton_Ulrich-Museum).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Albert was born on 17 May 1490 in Ansbach in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a>, the third son of Frederick I, Margrave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-ansbach">Brandenburg-Ansbach</a>. His mother, Sophia, was a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iv-jagiellon">Casimir IV Jagiellon</a>, so he was a nephew of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-i-the-old">Sigismund I of Poland</a> and a great-grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Władysław II Jagiełło</a>, who had defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> at Grunwald in 1410. He was raised for a career in the Church, spent time at the court of the Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> and became a canon of Cologne Cathedral. After the grand master Frederick of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> died in December 1510, Albert was chosen to succeed him early in 1511, in the hope that his kinship with Sigismund would help settle the dispute over eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>As grand master he refused to submit to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Polish crown</a>, and a war that began in December 1519 devastated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. A four-year truce followed in early 1521. At the Diet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> in 1522 he met the reformer Andreas Osiander, who won him over to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a>. At Wittenberg Martin Luther advised him to abandon the rules of his order, to marry and to convert Prussia into a hereditary duchy. Sigismund agreed, with Prussia to be a Polish fief, and the arrangement was confirmed by a treaty at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>. On 10 February 1525 Albert swore a personal oath to Sigismund and was invested with the duchy for himself and his heirs.</p><p>In 1526 he joined the League of Torgau, and he took part in plotting against Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> after the Augsburg Interim of 1548, though not prominently in the fighting. The lands and treasures of the church, which he confiscated, won over the nobles and paid for the court. He set up schools in every town and in 1544 founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-konigsberg">University of Königsberg</a>, where he appointed Osiander in 1549.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Osiander's quarrel with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-melanchthon">Philip Melanchthon</a> over justification by faith began the troubles of Albert's last years. With no church lands left to give, taxes rose and his rule became unpopular. After Osiander died in 1552 the duke favoured the preacher Johann Funck and the adventurer Paul Skalić. In 1566 the Estates appealed to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-ii-augustus">Sigismund II Augustus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, whose commission had Funck executed, while Skalić fled. Albert, virtually deprived of power, died at Tapiau on 20 March 1568 of the plague, with his wife. His son Albert Frederick succeeded him. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-konigsberg">University of Königsberg</a>, the Albertina, was the second Lutheran university in the German states, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marburg">Marburg</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Albert, Duke of Prussia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When <b>Albert of Brandenburg-Ansbach</b> secularised the remaining territory in 1525, the Duchy of Prussia became the first Lutheran state, and its inheritance by the Hohenzollern electors of Brandenburg in 1618 laid the foundation of the later Kingdom of Prussia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-the-teutonic-order">State of the Teutonic Order · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>From the mid-1520s rulers such as the elector of Saxony and Philip of Hesse organised Lutheran churches in their lands; in 1525 <b>Albert of Prussia</b> turned the lands of the Teutonic Order into a Lutheran duchy, and the kings of Sweden and Denmark followed.</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Bishop Erhard von Queis adopted Lutheranism and, after the secularisation of the Order's state into Ducal Prussia in 1525, surrendered his secular rule to <b>Duke Albert</b> in 1527.</blockquote><a href="/state/bishopric-of-pomesania">Bishopric of Pomesania</a></li><li><blockquote>Bishop Georg von Polentz adopted Lutheranism in 1523 and in 1525 transferred his secular rule to <b>Duke Albert</b> when the Order's state became Ducal Prussia, the first Protestant state.</blockquote><a href="/state/bishopric-of-samland">Bishopric of Samland</a></li><li><blockquote>When <b>Grand Master Albert</b> secularised Prussia in 1525, the order's German master, Walter von Cronberg, who resided at Mergentheim in Franconia, was made administrator of the grand mastership in 1527, and his successors combined both offices as Hoch- und Deutschmeister.</blockquote><a href="/state/teutonic-order-mergentheim">Teutonic Order Mergentheim</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ansbach, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Tapiau castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.3,10.58&amp;year=1540" aria-label="Ansbach on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4794 445 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Albert, Duke of Prussia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5244 715h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5244 715h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.3,10.58&amp;year=1540">What was here in 1540? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
